</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Ablation study on abrupt distribution shifts: shuffled vs. unshuffled source domains. <math id="S4.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{OOD}_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>OOD</mtext><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{OOD}_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy (%) is reported. TEA maintains generalization gains despite abrupt distribution changes and remains comparable to strong DG baselines like DiWA. ERM and DiWA are unaffected by smooth vs. abrupt shifts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abrupt and Unpredictable Shift Ablation.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 4.2 Ablation Study and Analysis ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ Scaling Up Temporal Domain Generalization via Temporal Experts Averaging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows results using shuffled source domains to simulate abrupt distribution shifts on Yearbook and FMoW. Theoretically, under abrupt shifts, TDG degrades to standard DG where TEA’s adaptive averaging gains should diminish while model averaging effects remain. Results align with theory: TEA with shuffled domains (TEA-S) performs worse than standard TEA but remains comparable with strong DG baseline DiWA, demonstrating robustness when temporal assumptions are violated.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T10" class="ltx_table">
